{"number":2,"title":"Addition (2-digit)","slug":"addition","color":"","level":"","estTime":"","description":"Class 2 IMO — Addition of 2-digit numbers with and without carrying, adding three numbers, and word problems with olympiad reasoning practice.","topics":[{"title":"Adding Without Carrying","concept":"<p>To add 2-digit numbers, add the <strong>ones</strong> first, then the <strong>tens</strong>. When the ones add up to less than 10, there is no carrying. For example, 23 + 14: ones 3 + 4 = 7, tens 2 + 1 = 3, so the answer is 37.</p><p>Addition can be done in any order: 23 + 14 is the same as 14 + 23.</p>","visual":null,"examples":[{"number":"1","problem":"Add: 42 + 35.","solution":"Ones 2 + 5 = 7, tens 4 + 3 = 7, so 42 + 35 = 77."},{"number":"2","problem":"Add: 51 + 6.","solution":"Ones 1 + 6 = 7, tens stays 5, so 51 + 6 = 57."}],"recap":["Add ones first, then tens.","Order does not change the sum: a + b = b + a."],"quiz":[{"q":"Add: 34 + 25 = ?","options":["59","69","57","49"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ones 4 + 5 = 9, tens 3 + 2 = 5, so 59.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"Add: 60 + 7 = ?","options":["13","67","76","70"],"correct":1,"explanation":"60 + 7 = 67.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"easy"}]},{"title":"Adding With Carrying","concept":"<p>When the ones add up to 10 or more, we <strong>carry</strong> the ten over to the tens place. For 28 + 16: ones 8 + 6 = 14, write 4 and carry 1 ten. Tens: 2 + 1 + 1 (carried) = 4, so the answer is 44.</p>","visual":null,"examples":[{"number":"1","problem":"Add: 47 + 38.","solution":"Ones 7 + 8 = 15, write 5 carry 1; tens 4 + 3 + 1 = 8, so 85."},{"number":"2","problem":"Add: 56 + 9.","solution":"Ones 6 + 9 = 15, write 5 carry 1; tens 5 + 1 = 6, so 65."}],"recap":["If ones make 10 or more, carry 1 to the tens.","Add the carried ten with the other tens."],"quiz":[{"q":"Add: 29 + 14 = ?","options":["33","43","44","42"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ones 9 + 4 = 13, write 3 carry 1; tens 2 + 1 + 1 = 4, so 43.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Add: 38 + 27 = ?","options":["55","65","66","64"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ones 8 + 7 = 15, write 5 carry 1; tens 3 + 2 + 1 = 6, so 65.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"}]},{"title":"Adding Three Numbers & Word Problems","concept":"<p>We can add <strong>three numbers</strong> by adding two of them first, then adding the third. In word problems, words like <em>in all</em>, <em>altogether</em> and <em>total</em> tell us to add.</p>","visual":null,"examples":[{"number":"1","problem":"Add: 12 + 13 + 5.","solution":"12 + 13 = 25, then 25 + 5 = 30."},{"number":"2","problem":"Riya has 24 stickers and gets 18 more. How many in all?","solution":"24 + 18 = 42 stickers."}],"recap":["Add three numbers two at a time.","'In all / altogether / total' means add."],"quiz":[{"q":"Add: 10 + 20 + 30 = ?","options":["50","60","70","40"],"correct":1,"explanation":"10 + 20 = 30, then 30 + 30 = 60.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"A box has 15 red and 17 blue balls. How many balls in all?","options":["22","32","33","31"],"correct":1,"explanation":"15 + 17 = 32 balls.","section":"everyday-math","difficulty":"medium"}]}],"tests":{"online":{"title":"","minutes":15,"questions":[{"q":"Which sum is the odd one out (three equal 40):  25 + 15, 30 + 10, 20 + 20, 22 + 19?","options":["25 + 15","30 + 10","20 + 20","22 + 19"],"correct":3,"explanation":"22 + 19 = 41; the other three equal 40.","section":"logical-reasoning","difficulty":"hard"},{"q":"If 3 + 4 = 7, then 30 + 40 = ___ ?","options":["7","34","70","700"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Tens add the same way: 30 + 40 = 70.","section":"logical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Fill the blank:  26 + ___ = 30 ?","options":["3","4","5","6"],"correct":1,"explanation":"26 + 4 = 30.","section":"logical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Add: 43 + 21 = ?","options":["64","63","65","62"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ones 3 + 1 = 4, tens 4 + 2 = 6, so 64.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"Add: 37 + 28 = ?","options":["55","65","64","66"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Ones 7 + 8 = 15, write 5 carry 1; tens 3 + 2 + 1 = 6, so 65.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Add: 19 + 19 = ?","options":["38","28","39","18"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Ones 9 + 9 = 18, write 8 carry 1; tens 1 + 1 + 1 = 3, so 38.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Add: 11 + 22 + 33 = ?","options":["55","66","65","56"],"correct":1,"explanation":"11 + 22 = 33, then 33 + 33 = 66.","section":"mathematical-reasoning","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Aarav read 18 pages in the morning and 23 pages at night. How many pages in all?","options":["41","31","40","42"],"correct":0,"explanation":"18 + 23 = 41 pages.","section":"everyday-math","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"A bus has 26 people. At a stop 14 more get in. How many people now?","options":["30","40","41","39"],"correct":1,"explanation":"26 + 14 = 40 people.","section":"everyday-math","difficulty":"medium"},{"q":"Meena buys sweets for ₹35 and a toy for ₹20. How much in all?","options":["₹45","₹55","₹50","₹65"],"correct":1,"explanation":"35 + 20 = ₹55.","section":"everyday-math","difficulty":"easy"},{"q":"A number added to 27 gives 50. What is the number?","options":["13","23","33","27"],"correct":1,"explanation":"50 − 27 = 23, so 27 + 23 = 50.","section":"achievers","difficulty":"hard"},{"q":"Three friends collected shells: 12, 15 and 13. How many shells altogether?","options":["38","40","41","39"],"correct":1,"explanation":"12 + 15 = 27, then 27 + 13 = 40 shells.","section":"achievers","difficulty":"hard"}]}}}